So his idea was to invent a computer language that&nbsp;
was really minimal and that had a minimal number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:11.120 --> 00:43:15.680
of instructions and he called it that because it&nbsp;
is a bit of a brain [&nbsp;__&nbsp;] to try and program and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:15.680 --> 00:43:20.000
it's very difficult to program it. But anyway,&nbsp;
the uh the reason that I used it was because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:20.000 --> 00:43:26.720
its minimalism. And and so you start off with&nbsp;
with very short random tapes uh that don't do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:26.720 --> 00:43:32.240
anything. But uh but this particular variation&nbsp;
of the language is one where the instructions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:32.240 --> 00:43:39.360
can self-modify. In other words, they can modify&nbsp;
the tapes themselves. Um but in the beginning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:39.360 --> 00:43:42.480
uh they don't do anything interesting because&nbsp;
they're they're just random instructions, right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:42.480 --> 00:43:47.520
They're they're like it's like a chemical soup of&nbsp;
just random molecules bumping around together. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:47.520 --> 00:43:54.640
procedure is as follows. You um you have a soup&nbsp;
of let's say a thousand of these um little tiny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:54.640 --> 00:44:00.560
uh random tapes. You take two of them out of the&nbsp;
