00:44:06.960 --> 00:44:11.760
And then uh once you've run them, you pull them&nbsp;
back apart and you put them back in the soup. and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:11.760 --> 00:44:16.560
and then you just do it again. You you mix and you&nbsp;
do it again. And you just do that over and over.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:16.560 --> 00:44:20.480
This doesn't seem like a like a very promising&nbsp;
way to make anything interesting happen. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:20.480 --> 00:44:24.960
take a bunch of really short random programs and&nbsp;
you know that that that have the potential to read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:24.960 --> 00:44:29.840
and write to themselves, but you know, that are&nbsp;
literally not doing anything uh of any interest in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:29.840 --> 00:44:34.080
the beginning and just do it over and over. What&nbsp;
do you expect to happen? Nothing. But what does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:34.080 --> 00:44:39.920
happen is actually really remarkable. So after&nbsp;
a few million interactions uh suddenly you begin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:39.920 --> 00:44:46.640
to see these uh tapes becoming longer and more&nbsp;
complex and they start to really compute. A lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:46.640 --> 00:44:53.280
of computation starts to happen and they start to&nbsp;
reproduce. Um why? Well um if you think about it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:53.280 --> 00:44:59.440
a program that replicates is going to out compete&nbsp;
a program that doesn't replicate. You know if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:59.440 --> 00:45:03.680
have a program that can replicate it will exist&nbsp;
in the future. Whereas if you have you know a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:03.680 --> 00:45:09.040
of code that can't replicate it will eventually&nbsp;
get overwritten by um uh by a program that can.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:09.040 --> 00:45:15.360
So you know in some very trivial way evolution&nbsp;
is already at work with respect to uh to to even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:15.360 --> 00:45:20.640
non-functional programs in the beginning because&nbsp;
uh anything that can replicate itself uh you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:20.640 --> 00:45:26.000
is going to persist into the future whereas&nbsp;
something that that can't won't. Uh so uh you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:26.000 --> 00:45:30.480
know it's it's a it's a sort of model for how&nbsp;
life arose and it shows you that life can arise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:30.480 --> 00:45:35.760
actually very easily in this minimal form and uh&nbsp;
I I should also point out that you know the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:35.760 --> 00:45:41.040
that these tapes began very short but that you you&nbsp;
stick them together in order to run them is really&nbsp;&nbsp;

